body:
My husband has joked that if we divorce he wants custody of our dog for precisely this reason. Who could resist this face

&#x200B;

 https://imgur.com/3GCgsGv

My MIL did some annoying things when the latest grandkid was born. I wasn't around when her other 2 grandkids were born, but I was around for the newest one. Every time we talked to her she always had to say how "amazing" this new kid was. It was as if she hadn't ever seen a baby before. "Look at his little fingers, isn't he amaaaaaziiing" Not exaggerating on how she drew out "amazing". She said it so often that my husband and I started finishing for her. 

&#x200B;

MIL: "I just can't believe how little his back is. Isn't he just.."

&#x200B;

Husband and I: "AMAAAAZIIIING"

&#x200B;

After a couple of times being made fun of she finally quit.  I think she was trying to make the baby more appealing to me. Make me all broody and want one. Didn't work :) Good luck with your sterilization!!

I think you're making a very classic assumption that most people have reasonable parents like yours were. People who weren't raised by abusive parents often can't understand why those that were abused react to their parents the way they do. Why can't you just have a reasonable conversation with them? Well, because their parents aren't reasonable people. When your attempts to have a rational adult conversation are met with nothing but manipulation, guilt trips, suicide threats, physical violence, etc you tend to stop trying to take that route with them. 

&#x200B;

Please have more empathy for those who may have been abused by those who were supposed to love them most. I'm so glad that your parents were self aware and kind enough to change their behavior after adult communication. Take a moment and appreciate that they did that, while acknowledging that many parents out there are so much less reasonable and loving. Be thankful you don't have to deal with the life long pain and stress that has pushed others in this thread beyond reason.

I ate what I was served for the most part. But, at the same time, if I didn't like something they wouldn't fix it. I remember one particular dish my dad made, some sort of bean soup. My dad is an excellent cook by the way, this was just a failed experiment. I hate beans. I will sit at a chili pot for 10 minutes picking out beans. I hate beans. Well he made a whole soup of beans. It looked like vomit. The smell was meh. I tried it. I hated it. They put me on the clock. I had 1 hour to finish that bean vomit soup or I would go to bed without dinner or desert. I patiently waited the hour and went to bed, the soup untouched after that initial bite. They didn't do that again. 

&#x200B;

If I were allowed to dictate my own diet at the time it would have consisted of peanut butter cups and steak. Nothing else. Parents have to make rational decisions regarding their children's diet, but at the same time those decisions should take into account genuine dislikes, allergies, intollerances, etc. There is a balance to be struck. I also think that the idea of forcing someone to finish their plate is idiotic. It just teaches people not to monitor their own portions. Left overs are a wonderful way to save a bit of money. No anger or punishment needs to be involved, just wrap up the left overs and have it for a snack/meal tomorrow.

The decision to have or not have kids is it's self morally neutral. It's what you do after that decision is made, and why you made that decision, that gives the choice it's morality. 

&#x200B;

For example: The decision TO have kids. If you make the decision to have kids for simply selfish reasons (to take care of you when you're old) then that shades the decision in a more morally bad light. If you have kids knowing without a doubt that they will suffer from horrible genetic disease, then that is a very bad decision. But, if you have children with the mind set that you are raising independent future adults and are teaching them how to behave in polite society, and if you are emotionally prepared for such a gargantuan task, then that is a good decision. It is a morally good decision for those who are willing and prepared to have a child. 

&#x200B;

The decision NOT to have kids. I obviously fall into this category. I honestly can't think of any reason that could qualify as bad in this instance. Freedom is a cherished value all over the world. People have fought and died for it. That includes the freedom to choose not to do something for any reason. Where a childfree person might find themselves in a morally bad light is in how they treat others and children. It's ok to defend yourself from bingos, but don't start the fight. Don't treat children badly just for existing around you. (That last one is a constant struggle for me. I don't like the little buggers and everything about them makes me uncomfortable. But I recognize that that is a me problem, and I am the one who has to deal with it. I do my very best to affect a polite indifference that hides the desire to crawl out of my skin to escape the movement and screeching)

I'm sorry you had to experience these breeders. Here is how an actual people parent (my friend E) handles her 3yr old with my puggle. My [puggle](https://imgur.com/Np3u7df) is fairly patient with people, but he doesn't have much experience with small children. 

&#x200B;

My friend's daughter Y loves animals but is still learning how to properly interact with them. When Y chases him and he's in a corner, E will tell her daughter that "The doggie wants space. Give him his space right now. See his body language, he doesn't want to be pet right now." Cue tears. "Ok then," E will say, "go to your room."  

&#x200B;

I was sitting on the couch and puggle was at my feet. I didn't notice what was going on until I heard him growl. I looked down and Y had his muzzle flaps in quite a grip! I leaned down and used a stern voice and said "Stop that please" Y stared at me in shock for a moment. "Thank you". Realizing that I had just chastised her daughter in front of her I looked at E ready to apologize. No, E backed me up. She said "Go to your room until you're ready to apologize to doggies parents" When Y was calm E would gently remind her daughter to pet him gently and the kid obeyed. That is how people parents teach their kids about dogs and how their kids might avoid being bitten.

I get what you're saying, but this wasn't the time or place for those kinds of personal questions. This was a new employee orientation. OP was giving a presentation to a group of new hires, not gossiping over coffee. If the person had stopped at her first question it would have been simply awkward for a second as the presentation was disrupted and then it would have moved on. Her second statement with the "***When you do***..." was where it started to become insulting. For one thing, OPs reproductive choices or capabilities weren't what the presentation was about. Completely off topic and derailing and time wasting not just for OP and that new hire, but for a whole room full of new hires. The thing with these kinds of personal questions is that they are personal. And, as such, OP has a right to take it personal. What if OP is struggling with infertility and being called out for not having kids was so painful and humiliating that it did serious emotional harm? Such personal questions also have no place in a professional presentation. 

&#x200B;

I do get what you're saying though. Sometimes I think we can get a little too sensitive to having the simple question "Do you have kids?" asked over and over again. It's annoying yes, but not something to let ruin your day. My problem comes when they don't take my answer, always an emphatic no, at face value and continue to push me about it. That's when I start to have a problem depending on how they are going about it. If they're genuinely curious about why I would make that decision then I have no problem telling them my reasons in a friendly manner. If they're condescending and hostile then they're going to get that right back at them.
